  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 241 PM EDT Fri Jun 12 2026\n\nEarly afternoon GOES water vapor imagery reveals a broad trough \nparked over far northern Ontario extending a weak shortwave into the \nUpper Great Lakes. Marginal synoptic support coupled with building \ndiurnal instability is working to touch off scattered showers across \nthe NW UP. Through the rest of the afternoon and into the early \nevening, expecting continued development of scattered \nshowers/thunderstorms into the east half. CAM guidance suggests \nincreased activity along the developing Lake Michigan lake breeze \nboundary seen in satellite imagery pushing into southern Luce \nCounty. Latest RAP analysis highlights meager 0-6 km shear <25 kts \nand growing MUCAPE between 500-1000 j/kg, so not thinking any severe \nstorms today, however, should a stronger storm latch onto localized \ngreater shear along a lake breeze boundary will not rule out a brief \nperiod of heavy rain and small sub-severe hail. Otherwise, afternoon \ntemps will peak in the upper 60s to low 70s amidst breezy WSW winds. \nTonight, overnight lows fall back into the 50s area wide, coolest \ninterior.\n\nA secondary shortwave traverses the region late tonight, allowing a \nswath of light rain to develop across north-central WI that may \ngraze the southern UP through day break tomorrow, though guidance \nhas ever so slightly trended this line south. By tomorrow afternoon, \nanother deeper wave/surface trough descends across Lake Superior, \nsupporting a cold front and northwesterly wind shift in the \nafternoon. Marginal diurnal instability and weak shear will once \nagain support scattered shower and thunderstorm development ahead of \nthis feature. Latest CAM ensembles suggests precip will be largely \nconfined to the east half by the mid to late afternoon. Breezy west \nwinds 25-35 mph will again be common similar to obs seen today, \nespecially in the Keweenaw, before veering WNW behind the cold \nfront. Cooler overnight temps in the 40s are expected overnight into \nSunday morning.\n\nDiurnally forced showers may develop Sunday thanks to this cooler \nairmass aloft, but probability is currently low (<25% chance). The \nnext rainfall opportunity comes Tuesday/Tuesday night, when another \npronounced shortwave digs through the Northern Tier into the Great \nLakes. Afterwards, there are indications that a vigorous wave and \nwidespread moderate to heavy rainfall event could move into the \nGreat Lakes region Wednesday night into Thursday. Individual global \nmodels and ensembles show subtle differences in track and strength",
